    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d487">

                <pb facs="0528" n="518" xml:id="pg518"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00112 European War 1939/10615: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-21T21:00:00+01:00">April 21, 1944—9
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 21—8:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1397. Department’s 635, April 10, 8 p.m.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d487-f1"/> Under
                    instructions from Soviet Government Semenov, Counselor of Soviet Legation here,
                    yesterday called on Boheman and supported American representation regarding
                    immediate cessation of Swedish exports of bearings, bearing machinery, et
                    cetera, and piston rings.</p>
                <p>In the ensuing conversation Semenov expressed opinion that Sweden has no real
                    reason to fear Germany and that discovery of war maps (Legation’s 1317, April
                        17<anchor n="24" xml:id="d487-f2"/>) was result of German plant which could
                    only be described as “a cheap bluff”.</p>
                <p>My 308, April 21, 9 p.m. repeats this to London.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
